WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:11.518
Zak and Alma waited in line at the ice cream vendor's cart.

2
00:00:12.318 --> 00:00:17.601
They were taking a break from racing around the park playing Dragons Search on their cell phones.

3
00:00:18.582 --> 00:00:24.145
They'd spent two hours trying to see who could find and capture the most dragons in the game.

4
00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:29.588
"You might have the higher score now, but I'm going to win," Zak teased.

5
00:00:31.176 --> 00:00:32.157
"I doubt that."

6
00:00:32.537 --> 00:00:33.338
Alma smiled.

7
00:00:33.358 --> 00:00:37.042
"There's no way you can catch up to my score!"

8
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:41.826
While Zak ordered, Alma noticed a man who looked familiar.

9
00:00:42.487 --> 00:00:45.289
The man was circling around, staring at his phone.

10
00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:50.014
"Is that our social studies teacher from last year, Mr.

11
00:00:50.054 --> 00:00:51.415
Perry?" she asked.

12
00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:54.658
Zak nodded as he paid for the ice cream.

13
00:01:00.216 --> 00:01:00.636
"Hey, Mr.

14
00:01:00.656 --> 00:01:01.977
Perry!" he called.

15
00:01:01.997 --> 00:01:05.019
"Phone away or it's mine for the day!"

16
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.262
Mr. Perry used to say that when he caught students using their phones in school.

17
00:01:12.203 --> 00:01:14.185
Mr. Perry looked up and smiled.

18
00:01:14.205 --> 00:01:16.086
"Hi, kids.

19
00:01:16.266 --> 00:01:17.207
I guess you caught me!"

20
00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:21.469
"I thought you didn't like cell phones," Alma said.

21
00:01:21.489 --> 00:01:24.811
"Not in school," Mr.

22
00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:25.672
Perry replied.

23
00:01:26.604 --> 00:01:30.606
"But I'm thinking I might use them to teach my next unit on reading different types of maps."

24
00:01:30.626 --> 00:01:33.528
"You mean, like using a map app?"

25
00:01:33.988 --> 00:01:34.709
Zak asked.

26
00:01:34.729 --> 00:01:38.831
"Actually, there's a new game all the kids are playing," Mr.

27
00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:39.811
Perry explained.

28
00:01:39.831 --> 00:01:45.374
"You have to capture dragons and—" "You mean Dragons Search?"

29
00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:47.676
Alma asked, showing him her phone.

30
00:01:47.696 --> 00:01:49.457
"That's it!"

31
00:01:49.757 --> 00:01:50.737
Mr. Perry said.

32
00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:55.240
"The game uses the Global Positioning System, or GPS.

33
00:01:55.956 --> 00:01:59.558
That's how the game knows where you're located and which dragons you can catch."

34
00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:04.220
"I know the game uses our location," Zak said.

35
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.061
"But I don't understand how GPS works."

36
00:02:07.081 --> 00:02:10.382
"I'm sure you're not alone in that," Mr.

37
00:02:10.422 --> 00:02:11.143
Perry said.

38
00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:15.985
"GPS relies on satellites twenty-thousand kilometers above Earth.

39
00:02:16.745 --> 00:02:22.768
The satellites orbit Earth, and at any given time or place, there are around nine satellites overhead."

40
00:02:24.588 --> 00:02:27.109
"But how does my phone communicate with them?"

41
00:02:27.669 --> 00:02:28.410
Alma asked.

42
00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.591
"The satellites regularly send signals with their position.

43
00:02:33.011 --> 00:02:36.953
The GPS receivers in our devices pick up those signals," Mr.

44
00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:37.693
Perry said.

45
00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:41.515
"Twenty-thousand kilometers above Earth!"

46
00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:42.936
Alma exclaimed.

47
00:02:42.956 --> 00:02:46.777
"It must take a long time for the signals to reach Earth."

48
00:02:46.797 --> 00:02:52.559
"Nope! The signals travel very fast—at the speed of light," Mr.

49
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:53.440
Perry replied.

50
00:02:54.292 --> 00:03:00.154
"Your device calculates the distance between the satellites to find the exact longitude and latitude of your location.

51
00:03:00.734 --> 00:03:05.155
The GPS receiver needs at least three satellite signals to calculate your position."

52
00:03:05.175 --> 00:03:10.577
"With so many satellites up in space, how do they know not to bump into each other?"

53
00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:11.717
Zak asked.

54
00:03:11.737 --> 00:03:14.478
"Good question," Mr.

55
00:03:14.518 --> 00:03:15.218
Perry said.

56
00:03:15.238 --> 00:03:20.420
"The satellites travel in a fixed orbit around Earth at a set rate of speed.

57
00:03:21.096 --> 00:03:24.959
The satellites and orbits are spaced to maintain a safe distance from each other.

58
00:03:25.539 --> 00:03:29.302
Each satellite takes approximately 12 hours to complete one orbit."

59
00:03:29.322 --> 00:03:33.785
"My cousins have a hobby they're always talking about," Zak said.

60
00:03:33.805 --> 00:03:36.587
"It has something to do with GPS.

61
00:03:37.047 --> 00:03:38.308
It starts with 'geo.'"

62
00:03:38.328 --> 00:03:41.931
"Geocaching?" Mr.

63
00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:42.751
Perry asked.

64
00:03:42.771 --> 00:03:44.132
"That's it!"

65
00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:49.076
Zak licked some ice cream as it started dripping.

66
00:03:50.048 --> 00:03:53.149
"Geocaching is like a giant treasure hunt," Mr.

67
00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:54.169
Perry explained.

68
00:03:54.189 --> 00:04:00.891
"It's become very popular—there are over one million geocaches hidden all over the world."

69
00:04:00.911 --> 00:04:03.152
"How do you play?"

70
00:04:03.532 --> 00:04:04.292
Alma asked.

71
00:04:04.312 --> 00:04:07.693
"Players choose a geocache from a list.

72
00:04:08.293 --> 00:04:13.455
Then, using the geocache's coordinates, they use GPS to find the geocache.

73
00:04:13.995 --> 00:04:17.736
It usually involves a fun hike or exploring an interesting neighborhood."

74
00:04:18.768 --> 00:04:21.330
"Sounds like Dragons Search," Alma said.

75
00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.251
"Kind of.

76
00:04:23.611 --> 00:04:27.973
Except geocaches are real boxes with real objects inside," Mr.

77
00:04:28.013 --> 00:04:28.974
Perry explained.

78
00:04:28.994 --> 00:04:33.656
"If you take something out of the box, you leave something of similar value behind.

79
00:04:34.397 --> 00:04:37.999
You also sign the logbook, leaving a note for other geocachers."

80
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.922
"We should ask your cousins to take us geocaching, Zak," Alma said.

81
00:04:43.942 --> 00:04:47.404
"Just remember to be respectful and safe.

82
00:04:48.512 --> 00:04:51.634
Don't roam onto private property or into dangerous terrain.

83
00:04:52.055 --> 00:04:56.158
And don't take unnecessary risks just to reach the geocache," Mr.

84
00:04:56.198 --> 00:04:56.898
Perry said.

85
00:04:56.918 --> 00:04:59.420
"Thanks for the lesson, Mr.

86
00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.701
Perry," Zak said.

87
00:05:00.721 --> 00:05:06.345
"When we get back to our game, I'm going to imagine signals bouncing between my phone and the

88
00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:07.586
GPS satellites."

89
00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:11.889
"Me too," Alma laughed.

90
00:05:12.569 --> 00:05:14.891
"That'll make beating Zak even more fun!"
